title: list --sort priority and pick disagree about which end is urgent
updated: 2026-08-10T04:23:40Z
---

Surfaced by the #66 fix, which corrected `list --sort priority` to follow the
configured priority order instead of alphabetical. Before the fix the output
happened to read critical-first, by accident of the alphabet. After it, the sort
follows config order — low, medium, high, critical — so the output flipped.

That is correct for a sort key, and it matches kanban-md. But it leaves a
visible asymmetry inside karr: `Cmd/Pick.pm` and `Cmd/Context.pm` still order by
`App::karr::Config->priority_order`, which is critical-first, because for
picking, urgency-first is the point.

So `karr list --sort priority` puts the least urgent task at the top while
`karr pick` takes the most urgent. Both defensible in isolation; together they
are a trap for anyone reading a list and assuming the top line is what pick
would take.

Decide one of:
- leave it, and document that --sort is ascending by the configured order while
  pick is urgency-first (cheapest, needs a doc line in the POD for list)
- make --sort priority descending by default, deviating from kanban-md
- give list an urgency-first shorthand so the intent is explicit

Whoever takes this: also check `--reverse` interacts sanely with the choice, and
that `karr board` (which groups by status) is not affected.
